<p class="lead">Independent contractor taxes catch a lot of new freelancers, drivers and consultants off guard. No one withholds anything from your checks, so you are responsible for paying Social Security, Medicare and income tax yourself, usually in four installments a year. This guide walks you through exactly what you owe, when it is due, which forms matter and how to keep more of what you earn.</p>

<div class="key-takeaways"><p class="kt-title">Key takeaways</p><ul>
<li>Contractors owe two federal taxes: 15.3% self-employment tax and regular income tax on net profit.</li>
<li>Estimated payments for 2026 income are due April 15, June 15 and September 15, 2026, and January 15, 2027.</li>
<li>For payments made in 2026, clients only issue a Form 1099-NEC at $2,000 or more, but every dollar is still taxable.</li>
<li>Deductions such as home office, mileage, software and half of your self-employment tax can lower your bill significantly.</li>
<li>Setting aside 25% to 30% of each payment is a simple habit that prevents April surprises.</li>
