<div class="callout"><p><strong>Note:</strong> This article is general information for the 2026 tax year, not tax advice. Rules change and every situation is different, so confirm details with the IRS or a qualified tax professional.</p></div>

<h2 id="who-counts-as-a-contractor">Who counts as an independent contractor?</h2>
<p>Your independent contractor tax responsibilities start with your classification. An independent contractor runs their own business and provides services to clients, rather than working under an employer's direction. The IRS looks at three broad areas: behavioral control (who decides how the work is done), financial control (who pays expenses, who can make or lose money) and the type of relationship (contracts, benefits, permanence).</p>
<p>The IRS explains these tests on its page about <a href="https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/independent-contractor-defined" target="_blank" rel="noopener">who counts as an independent contractor</a>. If you set your own hours, use your own tools, work for several clients and send invoices, you are probably a contractor. If a company trains you, sets your schedule and supervises your methods, you may actually be an employee, and the company should be withholding taxes.</p>
<p>Common examples of contract work include rideshare and delivery driving, freelance writing and design, IT consulting, handyman and trades work billed per job, and 1099 sales roles.</p>

<h2 id="how-independent-contractor-taxes-work">How independent contractor taxes work</h2>
<p>When you are an employee, your employer withholds income tax from every paycheck and pays half of your Social Security and Medicare. As a contractor you are both the worker and the business, so you handle all of it. Taxes for independent contractors fall into two buckets.</p>

<h3>1. Self-employment tax</h3>
<p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-employment_tax" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Self-employment tax</a> is how contractors pay into Social Security and Medicare. The rate is 15.3%, made up of 12.4% for Social Security and 2.9% for Medicare. It applies to 92.35% of your net self-employment earnings, and it kicks in once you have $400 or more of net profit for the year.</p>
<p>The Social Security portion stops once your combined wages and self-employment earnings hit the wage base, which is $184,500 for 2026. The Medicare portion has no cap, and an extra 0.9% Medicare tax applies to earnings above $200,000 for single filers. The good news: you can deduct half of your self-employment tax when figuring your income tax.</p>

<div class="stat-row">
<div class="stat"><strong>15.3%</strong><span>Self-employment tax rate</span></div>
<div class="stat"><strong>92.35%</strong><span>Share of net earnings it applies to</span></div>
<div class="stat"><strong>$184,500</strong><span>2026 Social Security wage base</span></div>
</div>

<h3>2. Federal income tax</h3>
<p>Your net profit (gross income minus business expenses) is added to any other income, such as a W-2 job. You then subtract deductions, including half of your self-employment tax, the standard deduction ($16,100 single or $32,200 married filing jointly for 2026) and, for many contractors, the 20% qualified business income (QBI) deduction. Whatever is left is taxed at the regular federal brackets.</p>
<p>If you live in Texas, there is no state personal income tax to add on top. Contractors in other states should check their state's estimated-tax rules too.</p>

<h2 id="forms-to-know">Tax forms every contractor should know</h2>
<p>Tax reporting for independent contractors involves a handful of forms. You do not need to memorize them, but you should know what each one does.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Who sends or files it</th><th>What it is for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>W-9</td><td>You give it to clients</td><td>Provides your name, address and taxpayer ID so clients can report what they paid you.</td></tr>
<tr><td>1099-NEC</td><td>Clients send it to you</td><td>Reports nonemployee pay. For payments made in 2026, required once a client pays you $2,000 or more (up from $600).</td></tr>
<tr><td>1099-K</td><td>Payment apps and marketplaces</td><td>Reports card and app payments. The federal threshold is over $20,000 and more than 200 transactions.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Schedule C</td><td>You file it</td><td>Calculates your business profit or loss: income minus expenses.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Schedule SE</td><td>You file it</td><td>Calculates your self-employment tax from your Schedule C profit.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Form 1040-ES</td><td>You use it</td><td>Worksheet and vouchers for quarterly estimated tax payments.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><p><strong>Watch out:</strong> The higher 1099-NEC threshold means many small clients will not send you a form at all. That does not make the income tax-free. Track every payment yourself, including cash, Zelle and checks.</p></div>

<h2 id="quarterly-estimated-taxes">Quarterly estimated taxes and deadlines</h2>
<p>One of the most common questions we hear is "when do contractors pay taxes?" The answer: throughout the year. The IRS expects you to pay as you earn, so contractors who expect to owe $1,000 or more for the year generally make four estimated payments. The periods are not equal quarters, which trips people up.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Income earned</th><th>Payment due</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>January 1 to March 31, 2026</td><td>April 15, 2026</td><td>Same day as your 2025 return</td></tr>
<tr><td>April 1 to May 31, 2026</td><td>June 15, 2026</td><td>Only a two-month period</td></tr>
<tr><td>June 1 to August 31, 2026</td><td>September 15, 2026</td><td>Three-month period</td></tr>
<tr><td>September 1 to December 31, 2026</td><td>January 15, 2027</td><td>Four-month period</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full year 2026</td><td>April 15, 2027</td><td>File Form 1040 with Schedules C and SE; pay any balance</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Clients who paid you $2,000 or more in 2026 must send your 1099-NEC by February 1, 2027 (January 31 falls on a Sunday). You can pay estimated tax online through IRS Direct Pay, your IRS online account or EFTPS. The IRS page on <a href="https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/estimated-taxes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">estimated taxes</a> lists every payment option.</p>

<h3>How to avoid the underpayment penalty</h3>
<p>You generally will not owe a penalty if your estimated payments and any withholding cover at least one of these "safe harbors":</p>
<ul>
<li>90% of your 2026 tax, or</li>
<li>100% of your 2025 tax (110% if your 2025 adjusted gross income was over $150,000).</li>
</ul>
<p>The prior-year rule is the easiest for new or uneven earners: divide last year's total tax by four and pay that each quarter. If you also hold a W-2 job, you can raise your withholding there instead, since withholding counts as if it were paid evenly through the year.</p>

<h2 id="calculation-example">Estimated tax calculation example</h2>
<p>Here is how independent contractor tax math works for a single filer in Texas with $72,000 in gross 1099 income and $12,000 in business expenses. Figures are rounded, and the income tax uses 2026 federal brackets.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Gross 1099 income</td><td>All client payments</td><td>$72,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Net profit</td><td>$72,000 minus $12,000 expenses</td><td>$60,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Net SE earnings</td><td>$60,000 x 92.35%</td><td>$55,410</td></tr>
<tr><td>Self-employment tax</td><td>$55,410 x 15.3%</td><td>$8,478</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deduct half of SE tax</td><td>$60,000 minus $4,239</td><td>$55,761 AGI</td></tr>
<tr><td>Standard deduction</td><td>$55,761 minus $16,100</td><td>$39,661</td></tr>
<tr><td>QBI deduction</td><td>Limited to 20% of $39,661</td><td>$7,932</td></tr>
<tr><td>Taxable income</td><td>$39,661 minus $7,932</td><td>$31,729</td></tr>
<tr><td>Federal income tax</td><td>10% and 12% brackets</td><td>about $3,559</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Total federal tax</strong></td><td>$8,478 + $3,559</td><td><strong>about $12,037</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Quarterly payment</strong></td><td>$12,037 divided by 4</td><td><strong>about $3,009</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Notice that self-employment tax is more than double the income tax here. That is typical at moderate incomes and is why contractor taxes feel heavier than taxes on a W-2 paycheck. This person's total works out to about 20% of net profit; setting aside 25% would leave a comfortable cushion.</p>

