<h2 id="deductions">Deductions that lower contractor taxes</h2>
<p>Every legitimate business expense reduces both your income tax and your self-employment tax, so good deductions are worth about twice as much to a contractor as they first appear. An expense must be ordinary and necessary for your line of work.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vehicle costs.</strong> Use the IRS standard mileage rate or actual expenses for business driving. Commuting to a regular workplace does not count, but driving between clients or job sites usually does.</li>
<li><strong>Home office.</strong> If you use part of your home regularly and exclusively for business, the simplified method lets you deduct $5 per square foot, up to 300 square feet ($1,500).</li>
<li><strong>Phone and internet.</strong> Deduct the business-use percentage.</li>
<li><strong>Equipment and software.</strong> Laptops, tools, design software, accounting apps and subscriptions you use for work.</li>
<li><strong>Platform and payment fees.</strong> Marketplace service fees and payment processing fees are deductible expenses.</li>
<li><strong>Health insurance.</strong> Self-employed people can often deduct premiums for themselves and their family if they are not eligible for an employer plan.</li>
<li><strong>Retirement contributions.</strong> A SEP IRA or solo 401(k) lets you shelter a meaningful share of profit from income tax.</li>
<li><strong>Education and supplies.</strong> Courses that maintain or improve skills in your current business, plus office and job supplies.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Tip from our team:</strong> Recruiters at Best Jobs Pro see a lot of people move between 1099 gigs and W-2 assignments in the same year. If that is you, keep your contract expenses in a separate account so they are easy to total at tax time.</p></div>

<h2 id="recordkeeping">Recordkeeping that keeps you audit-ready</h2>
<p>Strong records are the cheapest tax strategy there is. They help you claim every deduction and answer questions quickly if the IRS ever asks.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Open a separate bank account.</strong> Run all business income and expenses through it. Mixing personal and business money is the number one bookkeeping headache.</li>
<li><strong>Save every receipt digitally.</strong> Snap photos or forward email receipts to a dedicated folder or app.</li>
<li><strong>Log mileage as you go.</strong> Record date, destination, purpose and miles. A GPS mileage app makes this nearly automatic.</li>
<li><strong>Reconcile monthly.</strong> Spend 30 minutes each month matching deposits to invoices and categorizing expenses.</li>
<li><strong>Move your tax set-aside immediately.</strong> Transfer 25% to 30% of each payment into a savings account you do not touch.</li>
<li><strong>Keep records at least three years.</strong> The IRS generally has three years to review a return, longer in some cases.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="common-mistakes">Common independent contractor tax mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Skipping estimated payments.</strong> Waiting until April means a big bill plus penalties.</li>
<li><strong>Only reporting income shown on 1099s.</strong> The IRS expects all income to be reported, and the higher thresholds make this mistake easier to make.</li>
<li><strong>Forgetting self-employment tax.</strong> Budgeting only for income tax leaves you about 15% short.</li>
<li><strong>Overstating deductions.</strong> Personal expenses, commuting and 100% business use of a family car are red flags.</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring classification.</strong> If a company treats you like an employee but pays you on a 1099, you may be misclassified. You can ask the IRS for a determination using Form SS-8.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="gig-workers-and-mixed-income">Independent contractor taxes for gig workers and mixed income</h2>
<p>App-based drivers, shoppers and freelancers follow the same rules for contract work taxes as any other contractor and carry the same contractor tax responsibilities. Your platform may send a 1099-K or 1099-NEC, or nothing at all if you fall under the thresholds. Either way, use your own records and the platform's annual earnings summary to report gross income, then subtract expenses like mileage and fees.</p>
<p>If you have a W-2 job and contract work, your employer's withholding counts toward your total tax. Many people simply increase withholding on their W-4 to cover their side income and skip quarterly payments entirely. If you would rather avoid the paperwork altogether, temporary and contract-to-hire roles through a staffing agency are usually W-2, with taxes withheld for you. You can browse current <a href="/job-postings/">job postings</a> to see what is available near you.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-get-help">When to hire a tax professional</h2>
<p>Plenty of contractors with simple returns file on their own using tax software. Consider a CPA or enrolled agent if you:</p>
<ul>
<li>earn over roughly $100,000 or have big swings in income,</li>
<li>are thinking about forming an LLC or electing S corporation status,</li>
<li>have employees or subcontractors of your own,</li>
<li>work across several states, or</li>
<li>received a notice from the IRS.</li>
</ul>
<p>A good professional often pays for themselves through deductions and planning you would have missed.</p>

{{cta}}

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts on independent contractor taxes</h2>
<p>Independent contractor taxes come down to a few habits: understand that you owe both self-employment and income tax, set money aside from every payment, pay estimated tax by each quarterly deadline and keep clean records of income and expenses. Do that, and tax season becomes a formality instead of a crisis.</p>
